9754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559034 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559754 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9ca9e5c-13b4-5378-89a1-16e1132aad45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559757 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e291dcc7-8c1e-5891-b89d-054f30fdf260" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559758 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186559475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559758 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559758 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559758 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186559475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559758 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4124ec5-3e6f-5d7a-9254-21f518b1c2eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559784 #edb/ref 1071 "For an object moving in a straight line, its acceleration is the rate of change of its speed. What are the units of acceleration in terms of meters and seconds?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1fe48b5d-2d75-5bc6-88de-325b6398cfcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559784 #edb/ref 1074 "We *measure the change* in the speed, as *we change* the time. Consequently, any appropriate unit must take the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{unit of speed}}{\\text{unit of time}}.\n$$\n\nIn terms of meters and seconds, the unit for speed is $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}},$ and the unit for time is $\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the appropriate unit for acceleration is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}})}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nor, meters per square second." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559786 #edb/ref 1071 "$64\\times (-2)^{-3}=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21de0665-fffe-50d8-acfd-284a6430dcc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559786 #edb/ref 1074 "To evaluate the given expression, we use PEMDAS.\n\nNotice that $(-2)^{-3}=\\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}}.$ So, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64\\times (-2)^{-3} & = \\\\ 64\\times \\frac{1}{(-2)^{3}} & = \\\\ 64\\times \\frac{1}{(-8)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{64}{8} & = \\\\ -(64÷8) & = \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559802 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559802 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559802 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559802 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the solution to the equation $0.6y=-4.8?$\n\nThe solution is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559802 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4143dbb0-9a1f-50d1-bc72-9b5c0cbd50d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559802 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10$ to get rid of the decimals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.6y & =-4.8 \\\\ 10\\cdot (0.6y) & =10\\cdot (-4.8) \\\\ 6y & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the equation using the multiplication principle, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y & =-48 \\\\ \\frac{6y}{6} & =\\frac{-48}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{6}y}{\\cancel{6}} & =-8 \\\\ y & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559823 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559823 #edb/ref 1069 "q-214915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559823 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559823 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-p+7q=-4 \\\\ 10p-9q=-21\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe variable $p$ can be eliminated from the system using the following two steps:\n\n1\\. Multiply the first equation by $10$ and obtain\n\n  $$\n  \\begin{aligned}-10p+70q & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n  $$\n\n2\\. Add the multiplied equation to the second equation to get\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$=-61.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559823 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81d71337-d62e-547f-9ba2-a1325854394f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559823 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's follow the steps provided:\n\n**Step 1**: Multiplying the first equation by $10$ yields the following equivalent system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(\\,-p+7q=-4\\,)\\,\\times (10) \\\\ 10p-9q=-21\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-10p+70q=-40 \\\\ 10p-9q=-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we add these two equations together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\cancel{10p}+70q & =-40 \\\\ \\cancel{10p}-9q & =-21 \\\\ (70q-9q) & =(-40-21) \\\\ 61q & =-61\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78940" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559824 #edb/ref 1071 "$|-\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}|=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8404264d-3a64-5581-ad4c-0afe9c6e6c44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559824 #edb/ref 1074 "Firstly, since $|-a|=|a|,$ we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n|\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}|.\n$$\n\nThen, we distribute the absolute value over the multiplication and division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}| & = \\\\ \\frac{|4{c}^{2}|}{|7|} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{|7|} & = \\\\ \\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${c}^{2}\\ge 0$ for any real $c,$ we have that $|{c}^{2}|={c}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4|{c}^{2}|}{7} & =\\frac{4{c}^{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559830 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559830 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559830 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559830 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{LCM}(18,30).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559830 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "919304f4-9784-5406-8c58-3a53fea4edc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559830 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the lowest common multiple of two numbers, we first find the prime factorization of both numbers. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $18:$ \n\n![](images/9e/9e7bddde8a287f6224b3e79f9fdacc3ac22d6db5a021b85df287e5e5fa321932.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $18=2\\cdot {3}^{2}.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $30:$\n\n![](images/31/312d8c33355fb36260cce172ab20bf907637528d0a5d04dc4dd1eceb59d765dd.png)\n\n \nTherefore, $30=2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 30 & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in either factorization:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $2$\n\n- the highest power of $5$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest common multiple of $18$ and $30$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(18,30)=2\\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 5=90.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559857 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559857 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51850" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559857 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559857 #edb/ref 1071 "If $z$ varies directly with $w$ and $z=72$ when $w=9$, find the relationship between $w$ and $z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559857 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6b59906-d6e5-5bad-9d0f-b7c18e490304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559857 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $z$ varies directly with $w,$ we use the equation\n\n$$\nz=kw.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that $z=72$ when $w=9$ to find $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =9\\cdot k \\\\ \\frac{72}{9} & =k \\\\ 8 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nz=8w.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559871 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559871 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559871 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559871 #edb/ref 1071 "Peter pays $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a dollar for $\\frac{1}{15}$ of a pound of pumpkin seeds. How much should he pay for $3$ pounds of seeds?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559871 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0a4a8a4-751d-5872-ad1c-8d3477e4c365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559871 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine how much Peter should pay for $3$ pounds of seeds, we first find the unit rate of dollars per pound and then multiply it by $3$ pounds.\n\nTo find the unit rate of dollars per pound, we divide the number of dollars, $\\frac{3}{5},$ by the number of pounds, $\\frac{1}{15},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nDividing one fraction by another is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}÷\\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\times \\frac{15}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 15}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{5} \\\\  & =45÷5 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $9$ dollars per pound. To determine how much Peter should pay for $3$ pounds, we multiply the unit rate by $3$ pounds and get\n\n$$\n9\\times 3=27.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Peter must pay $27$ dollars for $3$ pounds of pumpkin seeds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559890 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186559746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559890 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559890 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559890 #edb/ref 1071 "A  recording studio charges musicians an initial fee of $$80$ to record an album. The studio also charges an additional $$50$ per hour.\n\nWhat expression gives the cost, in dollars, of recording an album,  in terms of the number of hours $h$ used?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559890 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdafad3a-7759-57a9-8969-df2e3e1dfbfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559890 #edb/ref 1074 "The fixed fee for recording an album is $$80.$ Hence,  $80$ should be a constant term.\n\nNow, if $h$ is the number of hours used to record an album, the cost  for $h$ hours of a recording must be $50h$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost, in dollars, of recording an album will be $50h+80.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559894 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "00ddeb7a-74b2-5bc8-804f-2e9a90acf07e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559894 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186559890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186559897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186559894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186